/-- Every native source/target type pair obeys the mathematical inverse law
in the target machine ring. Even values require DomainError; odd values return
a target-typed inverse. All inputs are included, including negative values and
values outside the target type's ordinary conversion range: remainder-to-type
is wrapping, so it must not be replaced by checked conversion. The world is
unchanged and termination is required. No callee laws are assumed.

Julia documentation and source: base/intfuncs.jl:344-370,373-382 at
f755f49a9e41380d7da126b3c1db9fe7f83dd027. -/
def InvmodTypeNativeRingLaw : Prop :=
  ∀ (W : Type) (world : W) (s t : IntTy) (n : JInt s),
    let observed := invmodType (invmodTypeBitsOps (World := W) s) n t world
    if n.val % 2 = 0 then
      ∃ message, observed = some (.error (.jl (.domainError message), world))
    else
      ∃ inverse : JInt t,
        observed = some (.ok (.native t inverse, world)) ∧
        (n.val * inverse.val - 1) % (2 ^ t.bits : Int) = 0

/-- The bottom-type overload cannot produce a value, and its failure retains
the invmod method identity for every operand and rest-tuple representation.
